{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-50135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/assets/65b74168f1165014df494c238dc400ab772f2c2486935ba6b78fdcbc6658b3b6.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b297f50510641e01534a2d5265fe0571622cafb992e5c0bb86f1c29165ae9d9"} {"question" "q-50135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/assets/3b77cdfa4958cd1892c31baf858b5f8cbe29e00e8e2a45b698a43c5ddae6198e.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the line $y=0,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on this line.\n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the line $y=0$ divide the plane into $2$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $2$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>0$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=y.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b297f50510641e01534a2d5265fe0571622cafb992e5c0bb86f1c29165ae9d9"} {"question" "q-50135" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28827b86733477c4332f6a7aa2557ed79ac32872e82bda1204b639352ef62638"} {"question" "q-50135" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b297f50510641e01534a2d5265fe0571622cafb992e5c0bb86f1c29165ae9d9"} {"question" "q-50135" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=x"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=y"] ["math" "{y}^{′}={e}^{xy}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}={y}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b297f50510641e01534a2d5265fe0571622cafb992e5c0bb86f1c29165ae9d9"} {"question" "q-50135" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{′}=y"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b297f50510641e01534a2d5265fe0571622cafb992e5c0bb86f1c29165ae9d9"} {"question" "q-50188" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/assets/09ba55ca21ef70904948bcf6d9c0787bee8f7836a233e0d7ce689549966120cf.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b297f50510641e01534a2d5265fe0571622cafb992e5c0bb86f1c29165ae9d9"} {"question" "q-50188" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/assets/0052e47a810dc6c82dd68ff9d0527a0b121f935d42067e063478f4f98817ffed.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $y=0,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines. \n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =3{x}^{2}{y}^{2} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =xy\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>0$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<0$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b297f50510641e01534a2d5265fe0571622cafb992e5c0bb86f1c29165ae9d9"} {"question" "q-50188" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28827b86733477c4332f6a7aa2557ed79ac32872e82bda1204b639352ef62638"} {"question" "q-50188" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b297f50510641e01534a2d5265fe0571622cafb992e5c0bb86f1c29165ae9d9"} {"question" "q-50188" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{′}=(x+1)y"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=x+y"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=x-y"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=xy"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b297f50510641e01534a2d5265fe0571622cafb992e5c0bb86f1c29165ae9d9"} {"question" "q-50188" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{′}=3{x}^{2}{y}^{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b297f50510641e01534a2d5265fe0571622cafb992e5c0bb86f1c29165ae9d9"} {"question" "q-50350" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/assets/2f577535c7e293a64139c0c68d2f09ea083041d806a634ae2d9a27055e96c00d.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=x+y+1$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{x+1}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{x-1}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b297f50510641e01534a2d5265fe0571622cafb992e5c0bb86f1c29165ae9d9"} {"question" "q-50350" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/assets/b8a9560870e28c751b8869e298ffabdbcc23eec720c7a1057d55032a62767183.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become vertical as $x\\to 1.$\n\n- The slopes of the slope field become horizontal at $y=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=x+y+1$ does not evaluate to zero on $y=0$ (the value depends on $x$).\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{x+1}$ evaluates to zero on $y=0,$ but \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{x+1}\\to \\frac{y}{2}<\\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 1.\n$$\n\n- Equation III gives the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y}{x-1}$ evaluates to zero on $y=0,$ and \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=|\\frac{y}{x-1}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b297f50510641e01534a2d5265fe0571622cafb992e5c0bb86f1c29165ae9d9"} {"question" "q-50350" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28827b86733477c4332f6a7aa2557ed79ac32872e82bda1204b639352ef62638"} {"question" "q-50350" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b297f50510641e01534a2d5265fe0571622cafb992e5c0bb86f1c29165ae9d9"} {"question" "q-50350" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b297f50510641e01534a2d5265fe0571622cafb992e5c0bb86f1c29165ae9d9"} {"question" "q-50350" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b297f50510641e01534a2d5265fe0571622cafb992e5c0bb86f1c29165ae9d9"} {"question" "q-80703" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/assets/da6318e1b1b8123845993da64cea95f640b0cb67ad94bb183bbbe9e5b1bd7b89.png)\n\nThe diagram shown above is the slope field for which of the following differential equations?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b297f50510641e01534a2d5265fe0571622cafb992e5c0bb86f1c29165ae9d9"} {"question" "q-80703" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/assets/8f8ffabcc5b03b1f1b5856f4eb187137d1bf84043e47a43f6777df7b267853ec.png)\n\n \n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- First, the slopes of the slope field are horizontal along the lines $x=0$ and $y=4,$ as shown above. Therefore, the right-hand side of the differential equation should evaluate to zero on both of these lines. \n\nOf the given equations, the only equations that satisfy this condition are the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =xy-4x=x(y-4) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =x(y-4)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Second, the lines $x=0$ and $y=4$ divide the plane into $4$ regions. The sign of the slopes in each of the $4$ regions are easily deduced by considering the behavior of the solution curves in these regions. This information is summarized in the following table:\n\n| $y>4$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $y<4$ | $↗$\n\n$(+)$ | $↘$\n\n$(-)$ |\n|  | $x<0$ | $x>0$ |\n\n \nOf the remaining options, the only one that gives this behavior is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=xy-4x=x(y-4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is ${y}^{′}=xy-4x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b297f50510641e01534a2d5265fe0571622cafb992e5c0bb86f1c29165ae9d9"} {"question" "q-80703" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28827b86733477c4332f6a7aa2557ed79ac32872e82bda1204b639352ef62638"} {"question" "q-80703" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b297f50510641e01534a2d5265fe0571622cafb992e5c0bb86f1c29165ae9d9"} {"question" "q-80703" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}^{′}=(y-4)^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=x(y-4)^{2}"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=x-4y"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=xy"] ["math" "{y}^{′}=xy-4x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b297f50510641e01534a2d5265fe0571622cafb992e5c0bb86f1c29165ae9d9"} {"question" "q-80703" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}^{′}=xy-4x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b297f50510641e01534a2d5265fe0571622cafb992e5c0bb86f1c29165ae9d9"} {"question" "q-120286" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/assets/e8b573c6c2c59123de46346ccfc717fa89a98f7838e643a5e82972d753383588.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y+1}{x}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b297f50510641e01534a2d5265fe0571622cafb992e5c0bb86f1c29165ae9d9"} {"question" "q-120286" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/assets/2f654bc96d27ac53dd501b10c5beb0d1c7d04ff4f643c5044692afac26ca2ba8.png)\n\nNotice the following properties of the given slope field:\n\n- For $x\\ne 0,$ the slopes of the slope field become horizontal at $y=0$ only.\n\n- For $y\\ne 0,$ the slopes of the slope field become vertical as $x\\to 0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each equation.\n\n- Equation I gives the required behavior. For $x\\ne 0,$ the right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}$ evaluates to zero on $y=0$ only, and for $y\\ne 0,$ we have \n\n$$\n|{y}^{′}|=|-\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x}|\\to \\infty \\,\\text{as}\\,x\\to 0.\n$$\n\n- Equation II does *not* give the required behavior. The right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}$ does not evaluate to zero on $y=0.$\n\n- Equation III does *not* give the required behavior. For $x\\ne 0,$ the right-hand side of ${y}^{′}=\\frac{y+1}{x}$ does not evaluate to zero on $y=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b297f50510641e01534a2d5265fe0571622cafb992e5c0bb86f1c29165ae9d9"} {"question" "q-120286" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "28827b86733477c4332f6a7aa2557ed79ac32872e82bda1204b639352ef62638"} {"question" "q-120286" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b297f50510641e01534a2d5265fe0571622cafb992e5c0bb86f1c29165ae9d9"} {"question" "q-120286" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b297f50510641e01534a2d5265fe0571622cafb992e5c0bb86f1c29165ae9d9"} {"question" "q-120286" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14069384/state.json" "file_sha256" "5b297f50510641e01534a2d5265fe0571622cafb992e5c0bb86f1c29165ae9d9"}] "usage" {"q-50350" []} "basis" 2415 "retractions" [[17592186172498 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/3350/Source/Images/q-50350.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could have generated the slope field shown above?\n\n1. $y^{′} = x + y + 1$\n2. $y^{′} = \\frac{y}{x + 1}$\n3. $y^{′} = \\frac{y}{x - 1}$"]] 